The concept: a comparative safety argument stating that if a process could occur only at or below some center-of-mass collision energy, and natural high-energy particle impacts already reach or exceed that energy at some observed rate, then that process must already be happening in nature at a comparable or greater rate without harmful effect. It takes as input an energy threshold for a hypothetical process and the known energy/flux of naturally occurring high-energy collisions, and outputs a conclusion ruling out dangerous or novel consequences of reaching that energy artificially. It applies whenever the natural collisions and the artificial ones produce results with the same relevant kinematic properties (not just the same energy), and its validity can break down when a difference in produced-particle motion or reference frame between the two cases changes how the outcome subsequently behaves.
